## What to return

- worth_updating is false when the issue already says what matters, or when what tfy has adds nothing a reader of the issue needs. That is common, and a fine answer.
- When it is true, prefer a comment: it adds what is new without touching what people wrote. For example: how many more people reported the problem and what they saw; the agreed scope and its acceptance criteria; the cause; the pull request that fixes it and what changes for users.
- Suggest a new title or description only when the issue is unclear or incomplete in a way a comment cannot fix: a title that doesn't say what is wrong, or a description without steps to reproduce. Keep everything the author wrote that is still true, keep their voice, and add rather than remove. Leave title or body empty to keep them as they are.
- Use only what is given here; never invent facts, versions or dates. Drafts are not decisions: when you use one, say it is planned or proposed.
- Write in the language of the issue, plainly: no preamble and no sign-off.{{if .Public}}
- The repository is **public**: anyone can read what you write. Do not quote the Slack messages or name the people who wrote them; say what was reported in general terms. Leave out anything internal: customer names, internal links or hostnames, credentials, private data.{{end}}

Return worth_updating, a one-sentence reason, and comment, title and body (empty strings for what you would leave as it is).
